<% content_for :title, "Architecture" %>

<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-12 prose prose-lg font-serif text-[--color-ink] leading-relaxed">
  <header class="border-b border-[--color-rule] pb-6 mb-10 not-prose">
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-[--color-ink-soft]">About</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl text-[--color-ink] mt-1">Architecture</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 italic text-[--color-ink-soft]">
      How the corpus is modelled, and how cross-references work across translations.
    </p>
  </header>

  <h2>The unified addressing model</h2>

  <p>
    Every text in Open Scriptorium &mdash; the Hebrew Bible, the Greek New
    Testament, the Septuagint, the apocrypha, Josephus, Philo, the Gospel of
    Thomas &mdash; lives in the same data model. There is no separate
    &ldquo;scripture table&rdquo; and &ldquo;commentary table.&rdquo;
    Instead, three layers cooperate:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Works</strong> are concrete documents: a translation, an
      original-language edition, a commentary, a lexicon. Each work has a
      license, a language, and a list of <em>work units</em>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Work units</strong> are the smallest addressable piece of any
      work. For scripture this is usually a verse; for Loeb commentary it
      might be a numbered section; for the Gospel of Thomas it&rsquo;s a
      logion. Every work unit carries the actual text body.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Canonical refs</strong> are the abstract concept of
      &ldquo;Genesis 1:1.&rdquo; Translations and editions of the same
      passage all align to the <em>same</em> canonical ref. This is what
      makes parallel reading and cross-referencing possible.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <figure class="not-prose my-10">
    <%= render "shared/diagram_addressing" %>
    <figcaption class="text-xs text-[--color-ink-soft] italic mt-2 text-center">
      Diagram: BSB Genesis 1:1, WLC Genesis 1:1, and the LXX all point at
      the same canonical ref. The canonical layer carries no text &mdash; it
      is the <em>address</em> shared between editions.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Cross-references at the canonical layer</h2>

  <p>
    Because canonical refs are the shared addressing scheme, cross-references
    live there too &mdash; not at the work level. A statement like
    <em>&ldquo;Jude 1:14 quotes 1 Enoch 1:9&rdquo;</em> is one row, language-
    independent and edition-independent. It applies to every translation of
    Jude and every translation of Enoch automatically.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each cross-reference stores its source and target as Postgres
    <code class="font-mono text-sm">int4range</code> values over the
    sequential <code>ord</code> column on canonical refs. With a GIST index,
    asking <em>&ldquo;what cross-references touch this verse?&rdquo;</em>
    is a single indexed lookup &mdash; whether the matching reference points
    at one verse, a chapter, or a whole book.
  </p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-10">
    <%= render "shared/diagram_xrefs" %>
    <figcaption class="text-xs text-[--color-ink-soft] italic mt-2 text-center">
      Diagram: a single GIST overlap query against
      <code class="font-mono">source_range &amp;&amp; int4range(N, N+1)</code>
      finds every cross-reference touching canonical ord N, including ones
      whose range covers an entire chapter or book.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Versification &mdash; we defer to Crosswire</h2>

  <p>
    Different traditions disagree about verse numbering. The Masoretic Text
    counts Psalm superscriptions as verse 1; the Septuagint numbers Psalms
    differently from chapter 9 onwards; Catholic and Orthodox canons
    structure several books distinctly. We don&rsquo;t pretend these
    differences don&rsquo;t exist, and we don&rsquo;t invent ad-hoc
    mappings.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each canonical work is tagged with a
    <strong>versification scheme</strong> taken from the Crosswire SWORD
    project&rsquo;s published v11n list (<code>KJV</code>, <code>NRSV</code>,
    <code>MT</code>, <code>LXX</code>, <code>Vulgate</code>,
    <code>Catholic</code>, <code>German</code>, <code>Synodal</code>,
    <code>Leningrad</code>, &hellip;). Imports preserve the source&rsquo;s
    native scheme. Divergences across schemes live in
    <code>versification_mappings</code>, populated only from Crosswire&rsquo;s
    authoritative tables.
  </p>

  <h2>Word-level alignment</h2>

  <p>
    Original-language texts (Hebrew, Greek) carry word-level data:
    inflected surface form, lemma, Strong&rsquo;s number, morphological
    parsing. Words in a translation can be aligned to source words via
    <strong>alignment groups</strong> &mdash; the same model used by the
    Macula Hebrew and Macula Greek projects.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each alignment group is a logical unit containing some source words and
    some target words, supporting one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-one,
    many-to-many, and null alignments uniformly. This is how
    interlinear-style displays and quotation matching (e.g. detecting NT
    quotations of the LXX) will be powered.
  </p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-10">
    <%= render "shared/diagram_alignment" %>
    <figcaption class="text-xs text-[--color-ink-soft] italic mt-2 text-center">
      Diagram: a many-to-many alignment group joining a Hebrew construct
      chain to its English rendering.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

